G1220:`Original: δηνάριον

Transliteration: dēnarion

Phonetic: day-nar'-ee-on

Thayer Definition: denarius = "containing ten"

1.  A Roman silver coin in NT time. It took its name from it being equal
    to ten "asses" , a number after 217 B.C. increased to sixteen (about
    3.898 grams or .1375 oz.). It was the principal silver coin of the
    Roman empire. From the parable of the labourers in the vineyard, it
    would seem that a denarius was then the ordinary pay for a day's
    wages. (Mat 20:2-13)

Origin: of Latin origin

TDNT entry: None

Part(s) of speech: Noun Neuter

Strong's Definition: Of Latin origin; a denarius (or ten asses): -
pence, penny [-worth].`,
