1.  a mountainous region, lying northeast of Palestine and west of
    Damascus. At the time when John the Baptist made his public
    appearance it was subject to Philip the tetrarch, son of Herod the
    Great, among the regions assigned to this prince after his father's
    death. It was brought under Jewish control by king Aristobulus
    around 100 B.C. Its inhabitants had been noted for robbery and
    skilful use of the bow.

Origin: of Hebrew origin H3195

TDNT entry: None

Part(s) of speech: Adjective

Strong's Definition: Of Hebrew origin [H3195]; Ituraea (that is, Jetur),
a region of Palestine: - Itura.`,
