G2647:`Original: καταλύω

Transliteration: kataluō

Phonetic: kat-al-oo'-o

Thayer Definition:

1.  to dissolve, disunite
    a.  (what has been joined together), to destroy, demolish
    b.  metaphorically to overthrow, i.e. render vain, deprive of
        success, bring to naught
        1.  to subvert, overthrow
            a.  of institutions, forms of government, laws, etc., to
                deprive of force, annul, abrogate, discard
    c.  of travellers, to halt on a journey, to put up, lodge (the
        figurative expression originating in the circumstance that, to
        put up for the night, the straps and packs of the beasts of
        burden are unbound and taken off; or, more correctly from the
        fact that the traveller's garments, tied up when he is on the
        journey, are unloosed at it end)

Origin: from G2596 and G3089

TDNT entry: 09:38,5

Part(s) of speech: Verb

Strong's Definition: From G2596 and G3089; to loosen down
(disintegrate), that is, (by implication) to demolish (literally or
figuratively); specifically (compare G2646) to halt for the night: -
destroy, dissolve, be guest, lodge, come to nought, overthrow, throw
down.`,
