G4232:`Original: πραιτώριον

Transliteration: praitōrion

Phonetic: prahee-to'-ree-on

Thayer Definition:

1.  "head-quarters" in a Roman camp, the tent of the commander-in-chief
2.  the palace in which the governor or procurator of a province
    resided, to which use the Romans were accustomed to appropriate the
    palaces already existing, and formerly dwelt in by kings or princes;
    at Jerusalem it was a magnificent palace which Herod the Great had
    built for himself, and which the Roman procurators seemed to have
    occupied whenever they came from Caesarea to Jerusalem to transact
    public business
3.  the camp of the Praetorian soldiers established by Tiberius

Origin: of Latin origin

TDNT entry: None

Part(s) of speech: Noun Neuter

Strong's Definition: Of Latin origin; the praetorium or governor's court
