G4624:`Original: σκανδαλίζω

Transliteration: skandalizō

Phonetic: skan-dal-id'-zo

Thayer Definition:

1.  to put a stumbling block or impediment in the way, upon which
    another may trip and fall, metaphorically to offend
    a.  to entice to sin
    b.  to cause a person to begin to distrust and desert one whom he
        ought to trust and obey
        1.  to cause to fall away
        2.  to be offended in one, i.e. to see in another what I
            disapprove of and what hinders me from acknowledging his
            authority
        3.  to cause one to judge unfavourably or unjustly of another
    c.  since one who stumbles or whose foot gets entangled feels
        annoyed
        1.  to cause one displeasure at a thing
        2.  to make indignant
        3.  to be displeased, indignant

Origin: from G4625

TDNT entry: 12:39,1

Part(s) of speech: Verb

Strong's Definition: To " scandalize" ; from G4625; to entrap, that is,
trip up (figuratively stumble [transitively] or entice to sin, apostasy
